    S3(A("COMP"), A("fp:and_all"), S6(A("CONS"), A("derive:admitted"), S4(A("COMP"), A("not"), A("derive:stratified"), S2(A("CONST"), S2(S2(A("A"), S3(A("minus"), A("B"), A("C"))), S2(A("C"), S3(A("proj"), A("A"), S2(N(1), N(2))))))), S4(A("COMP"), A("not"), A("derive:stratified"), S2(A("CONST"), S2(S2(A("X"), S3(A("count"), A("Y"), N(1))), S2(A("Y"), S3(A("proj"), A("X"), S2(N(1), N(2))))))), S4(A("COMP"), A("not"), A("derive:stratified"), S2(A("CONST"), S2(S2(A("P"), S3(A("pairwith"), A("Q"), A("v"))), S2(A("Q"), S3(A("proj"), A("P"), S2(N(1), N(2))))))), S4(A("COMP"), A("not"), A("derive:stratified"), S2(A("CONST"), S2(S2(A("G"), S5(A("calc"), A("H"), A("+"), N(1), N(2))), S2(A("H"), S3(A("proj"), A("G"), S2(N(2), N(3)))))))))),

"A WITNESS FOR THE SUBSET VERDICTS. The subset checker had no surface a person could read store-wide: its rows reach the commit gate and a per-entity screen, and the only law over them answers T or F. A change of the subset legs' carrier -- step triples lambda reassembles, or recipes emitted from the NORMA join path -- must be read as VERDICT IDENTITY, the same violation rows before and after, not as `nothing broke`. So the checker gets its witness, in the shape test:witnesses already renders: a pair of lists whose cat is what prints, and a law that holds when both are empty. The value is every cmd:sub_viols row rendered by system:show, so a row reads as <fact type, kind, subject>; the law is that there are none. `witness` on the base store prints `witness empty: subset-verdicts`; on a store with standing subset violations it prints each one. A migration is measured against this line, and every later change to constraint evaluation is visible on it.",
DEF("test:subset_clean",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("null"), A("cmd:sub_viols"))),
DEF("test:subset_viols_witness",
    S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), S2(A("ALPHA"), A("system:show")), A("cmd:sub_viols")), K(PHI()))),
"test:decided_ring holds decide:rst_rows to literal populations: r1 is irreflexive over two roles of A, r2 is asymmetric over a role of B and a role of C, r3 is irreflexive over one role, and u1 is a uniqueness, which is no ring. The rule names r2 and r3, in that order, and nothing else, and cmd:dec_rows makes each the row a write reports, <constraint, deontic, instance>.",
DEF("test:decided_ring",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"),
       S3(A("CONS"),
          S4(A("COMP"), A("cmd:dec_rows"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("ring-spans-one-type")), A("decide:rst_rows")),
             K(S4(S4(S2(A("r1"), A("IR")), S2(A("r2"), A("AS")), S2(A("r3"), A("IR")), S2(A("u1"), A("UC"))),
                  S3(S2(A("IR"), A("ring")), S2(A("AS"), A("ring")), S2(A("UC"), A("uniqueness"))),
                  S6(S2(A("r1"), A("a.1")), S2(A("r1"), A("a.2")), S2(A("r2"), A("b.1")), S2(A("r2"), A("b.2")), S2(A("r3"), A("a.1")), S2(A("u1"), A("a.1"))),
                  S4(S2(A("A"), A("a.1")), S2(A("A"), A("a.2")), S2(A("B"), A("b.1")), S2(A("C"), A("b.2")))))),
          K(S2(S3(A("ring-spans-one-type"), A("deontic"), A("r2")), S3(A("ring-spans-one-type"), A("deontic"), A("r3"))))))),
"test:decided_uniqueness holds both to literal descriptors. T3a is a ternary with a uniqueness over one role, T4 a quaternary with one over two, and T3b, B2 and U1 meet n-1, so the first answers T3a and T4. Birth objectifies an n:1 binary, Visit an m:n one, and Flag a unary, and Ghost has no descriptor, so the second answers Birth.",
