DEF("test:writable",
    S4(A("COMP"), A("null"), A("main:alethic_of"), A("ui:violations"))),
"AND A LAW THAT STOPS EVERY WRITE SHOULD SAY WHAT STOPPED IT. writable answers T or F and nothing else, and F means Thm 1 refuses every successor -- a TOTAL write outage, not a degraded path. The case: a transition is removed from its machine, and readings/ui.md goes on saying `Transition 'redraft' has Event Label 'Redraft'`. That sentence MINTS the Transition, which then has no machine, no from, no to and no trigger -- four unfilled mandatories, four alethic violations, and every POST answering 409. A model check reports 0 errors, correctly: it validates the MODEL and an instance with unfilled mandatory roles is a POPULATION fact. This law is the one that sees it, and its witness answers the two lists a reader needs -- the SUBJECTS that make the store unwritable, then the FACT TYPES whose mandatory roles they leave unfilled -- so `witness` prints `WITNESS writable: redraft, TransitionIsDefinedInStateMachineDefinition, TransitionIsFromStatus, ...` instead of nothing. The law's own verdict is untouched: only the witness is added, so no report and no golden moves.",
"AND THE REAL LESSON IS ABOUT WHICH GATE AN AUTHOR RUNS. The law catches this the moment it is written, if the law report runs between the edit and the write: a check of the readings alone cannot see a population. The UI facts also live in a DIFFERENT FILE from the declaration, so removing the declaration left ui.md internally consistent and a model check happy to mint the subject. Grep the name across every reading before deleting a transition, a status or an event type -- and read the witness, which is now worth reading.",
DEF("test:writable_witness",
    S3(A("CONS"),
       S4(A("COMP"), A("theta:dedup"), S2(A("ALPHA"), N(3)),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("main:alethic_of"), A("ui:violations"))),
       S4(A("COMP"), A("theta:dedup"), S2(A("ALPHA"), N(1)),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("main:alethic_of"), A("ui:violations"))))),
