"EVERY MARKER IS A CLAIM THAT A RULE EXISTS, so this spans all four modes and not only the two that are obviously derived. Halpin: 'Derivation rules should normally be biconditionals (i.e., their main operator is iff). If their main operator is if, the fact type is only partly derived.' So `semi` (+) does not mean fewer rules, it means the rule's main operator is `if` -- and a semi derived fact type with no deliverer is a marking about an operator that is not there. It fails softly where `full` fails loudly: the asserted half populates, so the derived fact type looks alive while its derived half silently never arrives. `subtype` is the same claim in subtype form, since the marked reading IS the defining rule (* Each X is a Y that ...), so a derived subtype with no deliverer says a membership is computed and then computes nothing. Written as the first half of test:marker_known, which already counted every mode as a KNOWN name while only two were required to be a DELIVERED one.",
DEF("test:marker_marked",
    S3(A("COMP"), S2(A("ALPHA"), N(1)), S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:unfold_pairs"), S3(A("COMP"), A("ast:fetch"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("state:derived")), A("id")))))),
DEF("test:marker_dfts",
    A("derive:rule_heads")),
