"THE OBSERVED-FACTS LAW: A CASE SHOWS THE FACTS ITS OBSERVATIONS ARE OF, ONE SECTION PER FACT TYPE. A case is a Case whose Observations are of facts, by `Observation is of Fact`, and no reading is invented around Case. The fixture states its facts as an app does: Case c1 observes the Observations o1 and o2, o1 is of the Facts f1 and f2, o2 of f3, and o9, which c1 does not observe, of f9; each Fact is of its Fact Type and fills its Roles, and each RoleInstance, named `<fact>.<role>` as every objectified instance is, uses its Object Type Instance. f1 says Log Entry le1 has Call Outcome failed, f3 that le2 has not found, f2 that Cancel Request cr1 has PostHog Session Recording rec1, f9 that le9 failed. ui:facts of c1, its relation section aside, holds a section titled Log Entry has Call Outcome whose items link Log Entry le1 and le2, a section titled Cancel Request has PostHog Session Recording whose one item links Cancel Request cr1, and no item that links le9. The detail view showed the two Observations and stopped there, so the law answered F before the fix.",
