"A RETRACT OF AN ENTERED VALUE OF A ++ DERIVED FACT TYPE LEAVES WHAT ITS RULES DERIVE, AND NOTHING STORED. Over test:pp_entered, after a fact DELETE of law-pp-e3 for law-pp-k3 in LawPpOwn: a read answers law-pp-b1 for law-pp-k1 and law-pp-b2 for law-pp-k2, the rule`s rows, and nothing else; and the store holds none of the three. A ++ fact type is semi-derived as a + one is: what was entered of it united with what its rules derive, an entered row overriding nothing (core.md:380-382 and 395-397), and its storage decides when a derivation runs, never what it answers, nothing evaluating eagerly (core.md:391-394: no write stores a derived row). So the law enters for law-pp-k3, a key the rule derives nothing for, and a value for law-pp-k1 is refused by the Thing`s uniqueness. Run first, it failed: a ++ fact type was read as its rules alone, and a write stored the rows its rules derived as entered.",
DEF("test:plusplus_retracted",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("fp:and_all"), S3(A("COMP"), S5(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), A("test:rows_eq"), S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), A("system:pop_rows"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("LawPpOwn")), A("id"))), K(S2(S2(A("law-pp-k1"), A("law-pp-b1")), S2(A("law-pp-k2"), A("law-pp-b2")))))), S4(A("COMP"), A("not"), A("theta:member"), S3(A("CONS"), K(S2(A("law-pp-k3"), A("law-pp-e3"))), S3(A("COMP"), A("test:pr_stored"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("LawPpOwn")), A("id"))))), S4(A("COMP"), A("not"), A("theta:member"), S3(A("CONS"), K(S2(A("law-pp-k1"), A("law-pp-b1"))), S3(A("COMP"), A("test:pr_stored"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("LawPpOwn")), A("id"))))), S4(A("COMP"), A("not"), A("theta:member"), S3(A("CONS"), K(S2(A("law-pp-k2"), A("law-pp-b2"))), S3(A("COMP"), A("test:pr_stored"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("LawPpOwn")), A("id")))))), A("test:pp_retracted")))),
"A SYNC REPLACES AN ENTERED VALUE OF A FEDERATED ++ FACT TYPE, AND NOTHING DERIVED REPLACES ONE THE USER OWNS. A sync from an external federation is a derivation; for a federated fact type the next sync replaces an entered value; for one the user owns, nothing derived ever overwrites an entered value. Over test:pp_both, where law-pp-e3 is entered for law-pp-k3 in LawPpFed, federated, and in LawPpOwn, not, a sync of a page that reads law-pp-s3 for law-pp-k3 in both: a read of LawPpFed answers the page`s law-pp-s3 for law-pp-k3 and not the entered law-pp-e3, and still law-pp-b2 for law-pp-k2, which its rule derives; the store holds law-pp-s3 for it and not law-pp-e3; while LawPpOwn still answers law-pp-e3 for law-pp-k3, not the page`s law-pp-s3, and the rule`s law-pp-b1 for law-pp-k1 beside it. A ++ fact type is semi-derived as a + one is: what was entered of it united with what its rules derive, an entered row overriding nothing (core.md:380-382 and 395-397), and its storage decides when a derivation runs, never what it answers, nothing evaluating eagerly (core.md:391-394: no write stores a derived row). So the law enters for law-pp-k3, a key the rule derives nothing for, and a value for law-pp-k1 is refused by the Thing`s uniqueness. Run first, it failed: a ++ fact type was read as its rules alone, and a write stored the rows its rules derived as entered.",
DEF("test:plusplus_federated",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("fp:and_all"), S3(A("COMP"), S9(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:member"), S3(A("CONS"), K(S2(A("law-pp-k3"), A("law-pp-s3"))), S3(A("COMP"), A("system:pop_rows"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("LawPpFed")), A("id"))))), S4(A("COMP"), A("not"), A("theta:member"), S3(A("CONS"), K(S2(A("law-pp-k3"), A("law-pp-e3"))), S3(A("COMP"), A("system:pop_rows"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("LawPpFed")), A("id"))))), S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:member"), S3(A("CONS"), K(S2(A("law-pp-k2"), A("law-pp-b2"))), S3(A("COMP"), A("system:pop_rows"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("LawPpFed")), A("id"))))), S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:member"), S3(A("CONS"), K(S2(A("law-pp-k3"), A("law-pp-s3"))), S3(A("COMP"), A("test:pr_stored"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("LawPpFed")), A("id"))))), S4(A("COMP"), A("not"), A("theta:member"), S3(A("CONS"), K(S2(A("law-pp-k3"), A("law-pp-e3"))), S3(A("COMP"), A("test:pr_stored"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("LawPpFed")), A("id"))))), S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:member"), S3(A("CONS"), K(S2(A("law-pp-k3"), A("law-pp-e3"))), S3(A("COMP"), A("system:pop_rows"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("LawPpOwn")), A("id"))))), S4(A("COMP"), A("not"), A("theta:member"), S3(A("CONS"), K(S2(A("law-pp-k3"), A("law-pp-s3"))), S3(A("COMP"), A("system:pop_rows"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("LawPpOwn")), A("id"))))), S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:member"), S3(A("CONS"), K(S2(A("law-pp-k1"), A("law-pp-b1"))), S3(A("COMP"), A("system:pop_rows"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("LawPpOwn")), A("id")))))), A("test:pp_synced")))),
"<store>: the composed store with LawOgSrc, a Thing with a Basis, asserted; LawOgPlus, a Thing with a Value, marked + and derived as the projection of LawOgSrc; and LawOgStar, a Thing with a Value, marked * and derived as the projection of LawOgPlus; each unique on the Thing. No derived fact type the store keeps reads either, so the store computes both on read.",
DEF("test:og_schema",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("test:w2_ext"), S3(A("CONS"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("test:w2_ext"), S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("test:w2_ext"), S3(A("CONS"),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("test:w2_ext"), S3(A("CONS"), A("id"),
                K(S2(A("state:fts"), S3(
                   S5(A("LawOgSrc"), S2(A("Thing"), A("Basis")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(), PHI()),
                   S5(A("LawOgPlus"), S2(A("Thing"), A("Value")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(), PHI()),
                   S5(A("LawOgStar"), S2(A("Thing"), A("Value")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(), PHI())))))),
             K(S2(A("state:declared"), S3(
                S2(A("LawOgSrc"), S2(A("Thing"), A("Basis"))),
                S2(A("LawOgPlus"), S2(A("Thing"), A("Value"))),
                S2(A("LawOgStar"), S2(A("Thing"), A("Value")))))))),
          K(S2(A("state:derived"), S2(S2(A("LawOgPlus"), A("semi")), S2(A("LawOgStar"), A("full"))))))),
       K(S2(A("state:rules"), S2(
          S3(A("LawOgPlus"), S2(A("Thing"), A("Value")), S3(A("proj"), A("LawOgSrc"), S2(N(1), N(2)))),
          S3(A("LawOgStar"), S2(A("Thing"), A("Value")), S3(A("proj"), A("LawOgPlus"), S2(N(1), N(2)))))))))),
"The asserted rows of the on-read guard fixture, each <fact type, row>: law-og-k1 with the Basis law-og-b1, law-og-k2 with law-og-b2.",
DEF("test:og_rows",
    K(S2(
       S2(A("LawOgSrc"), S2(A("law-og-k1"), A("law-og-b1"))),
       S2(A("LawOgSrc"), S2(A("law-og-k2"), A("law-og-b2")))))),
"<store> to the fixture, its rows put over its schema and closed from it as a write closes, then a fact POST of law-og-e1 for law-og-k1 in LawOgPlus.",
DEF("test:og_entered",
    S4(A("COMP"), N(3), A("main:post_answer"), S4(A("CONS"),
       S3(A("COMP"),
          S3(A("COMP"), A("store:closed_from"), S3(A("CONS"), A("id"), S4(A("COMP"), S2(A("INSERT"), A("test:dft_put")), A("apndr"), S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), A("reverse"), A("test:og_rows")), A("id"))))),
          A("test:og_schema")),
       K(A("LawOgPlus")), K(S2(A("law-og-k3"), A("law-og-e3")))))),
"A RULE THAT READS A + DERIVED FACT TYPE ON READ SEES WHAT WAS ENTERED AND WHAT ITS RULES DERIVE (core.md:380-382). Over test:og_entered, where law-og-e3 is entered for law-og-k3 in LawOgPlus, a key its rule derives nothing for: a read of LawOgStar, a rule over LawOgPlus, answers law-og-e3 for law-og-k3 beside the derived law-og-b1 and law-og-b2, and a read of LawOgPlus the same.",
DEF("test:prg_union",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("fp:and_all"), S3(A("COMP"), S6(A("CONS"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:member"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("LawOgStar")), A("derive:or_dfts"))),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:member"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("LawOgPlus")), A("derive:or_dfts"))),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("test:rows_eq"), S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), A("system:pop_rows"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("LawOgStar")), A("id"))), K(S3(S2(A("law-og-k3"), A("law-og-e3")), S2(A("law-og-k1"), A("law-og-b1")), S2(A("law-og-k2"), A("law-og-b2")))))),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:member"), S3(A("CONS"), K(S2(A("law-og-k3"), A("law-og-e3"))), S3(A("COMP"), A("system:pop_rows"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("LawOgStar")), A("id"))))),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("test:rows_eq"), S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), A("system:pop_rows"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("LawOgPlus")), A("id"))), K(S3(S2(A("law-og-k3"), A("law-og-e3")), S2(A("law-og-k1"), A("law-og-b1")), S2(A("law-og-k2"), A("law-og-b2"))))))),
       A("test:og_entered")))),
"The store test:og_entered posts to.",
DEF("test:og_fx0",
    S3(A("COMP"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("store:closed_from"), S3(A("CONS"), A("id"), S4(A("COMP"), S2(A("INSERT"), A("test:dft_put")), A("apndr"), S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), A("reverse"), A("test:og_rows")), A("id"))))),
       A("test:og_schema"))),
"And a POST of law-og-e1 for law-og-k1, a key the rule derives law-og-b1 for, overrides no derived value (core.md:395-396): the Thing`s uniqueness refuses it, 409.",
DEF("test:og_refused",
    S3(A("COMP"), S3(A("COMP"), A("fp:and_all"), S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"), N(2), K(N(409)))), S3(A("COMP"), A("cn:contains"), S3(A("CONS"), N(1), K(A("Uniqueness violation")))))), S3(A("COMP"), A("main:post_answer"), S4(A("CONS"), A("test:og_fx0"), K(A("LawOgPlus")), K(S2(A("law-og-k1"), A("law-og-e1"))))))),
DEF("test:plus_read_guard",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("fp:and_all"), S3(A("CONS"), A("test:prg_union"), A("test:og_refused")))),

"EVERY TABLE THE MAP NAMES HAS A KEY. federation.md declares `Response is an entity type.` with no reference mode and no supertype, so the map gave it a table of its own and no primary key, rmap:keyed dropped it from rmap:coltabs, and rmap:unkeyed named it where no host prints it; the DDL never created Response and the write-back inserted into it. Every other entity type of the metamodel identifies through Function(.id), by a reference mode or by a declared subtyping. Over the composed base store rmap:unkeyed answers nothing. Before the map gave Response an identity it answered <Response, Response>, and this answered F.",
DEF("test:unkeyed_none",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("null"), A("rmap:unkeyed"))),

"test:tab_facts answers the fact types the columns of one table walk: operand <table, rmap:ctab>, each entry <concept, table, columns>, each column <owner, flag, path>, each step of a path carrying its fact types at 5, the empty list for an assimilation step.",
DEF("test:tab_facts",
    S4(A("COMP"), A("theta:flatten"),
       S2(A("ALPHA"), S4(A("COMP"), A("theta:flatten"),
          S2(A("ALPHA"), S4(A("COMP"), A("theta:flatten"),
             S2(A("ALPHA"), S4(A("COND"), S3(A("COMP"), A("lt"), S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), A("length"), A("id")), K(N(5)))), K(PHI()), S4(A("COND"), S3(A("COMP"), A("atom"), N(5)), K(PHI()), N(5)))),
             N(3))),
          N(3))),
       S4(A("COMP"), A("theta:flatten"),
          S2(A("ALPHA"), S4(A("COND"), S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), N(2), N(2)), N(1))), S2(A("CONS"), N(2)), K(PHI()))),
          A("distl")))),

"A RESPONSE IS AN OBJECT TYPE INSTANCE, SO ITS FACTS LAND WHERE A FAILURE`S DO. No reading says `Response is a subtype of Object Type Instance.`: all objects are object type instances. An entity type with no identification of its own identifies as every object does, through Function(.id), its instances Object Type Instances, so the map absorbs it as it absorbs a declared subtype of Object Type Instance (Failure, outcomes.md). Over the composed base store: Function is a keyed table, no table is named Response, and the four fact types federation.md declares for Response -- answers Function, is from URL, has HTTP Status Code, occurred at Timestamp -- are walked by columns of the Function table. Before the map gave Response an identity, Response was a table of its own, and this answered F.",
DEF("test:response_keyed",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("fp:and_all"), S4(A("CONS"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:member"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("Function")), S3(A("COMP"), S2(A("ALPHA"), N(2)), A("rmap:pkrows")))),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("not"), S3(A("COMP"), A("theta:member"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("Response")), S3(A("COMP"), S2(A("ALPHA"), N(1)), A("rmap:ctab"))))),
       S5(A("COMP"), A("fp:and_all"), S2(A("ALPHA"), A("theta:member")), A("distr"),
          S3(A("CONS"),
             K(S4(A("ResponseAnswersFunction"), A("ResponseIsFromURL"), A("ResponseHasHTTPStatusCode"), A("ResponseOccurredAtTimestamp"))),
             S3(A("COMP"), A("test:tab_facts"), S3(A("CONS"), K(A("Function")), A("rmap:ctab")))))))),

"A WRITE-BACK NAMES ONLY TABLES THE DDL CREATED. Two checks. Over a literal map of two tables, Kept with a primary key and Dropped with none, the tables the write-back walks are Kept alone: a table without a key is one rmap:coltabs leaves out and the DDL never creates, so the write-back may not name it either. And over the composed base store every table rmap:writeback walks is one rmap:coltabs creates. While rmap:writeback was rmap:ctab, the walk host.js emitToDb made, the first answered Kept and Dropped, the second named Response, and this answered F. Since a view derived fact type is not in rmap:coltabs, rmap:writeback reads rmap:coltabs as well, so the literal map holds a kept rmap:coltabs; it names Dropped too, so the key gate is still what leaves Dropped out.",
DEF("test:writeback_keyed",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("fp:and_all"), S3(A("CONS"),
       S3(A("COMP"), A("eq"), S3(A("CONS"),
          S3(A("COMP"), S2(A("ALPHA"), N(1)), S3(A("COMP"), A("rmap:writeback"),
             K(S3(S3(A("CELL"), A("stored:rmap:ctab"), S2(S3(A("Kept"), A("Kept"), PHI()), S3(A("Dropped"), A("Dropped"), PHI()))),
                  S3(A("CELL"), A("stored:rmap:pkrows"), S1(S4(A("pk"), A("Kept"), PHI(), A("Kept_PK")))),
                  S3(A("CELL"), A("stored:rmap:coltabs"), S2(S2(A("Kept"), PHI()), S2(A("Dropped"), PHI()))))))),
          K(S1(A("Kept"))))),
       S5(A("COMP"), A("fp:and_all"), S2(A("ALPHA"), A("theta:member")), A("distr"),
          S3(A("CONS"), S3(A("COMP"), S2(A("ALPHA"), N(2)), A("rmap:writeback")), S3(A("COMP"), S2(A("ALPHA"), N(1)), A("rmap:coltabs"))))))),

"THE COMPOUND FIXTURE: one file declaring Thing(.Aisle, .Bay), Aisle and Color value types, Bay declared nowhere, and Thing has at most one Color. test:ck_run is <cells, findings> as compile:check answers them for it, test:ck_store its cells as a store.",
DEF("test:ck_files",
    K(S1(S3(A("t.md"), A("Aisle is a value type.\nColor is a value type.\nThing(.Aisle, .Bay) is an entity type.\nThing has Color.\n  Each Thing has at most one Color.\n"), A("cx/t.md"))))),
DEF("test:ck_run",
    S4(A("COMP"), S3(A("CONS"), A("compile:of_x"), A("compile:findings_of_x")), A("compile:x"), S3(A("COMP"), S2(A("ALPHA"), A("test:ln_pair")), A("test:ck_files")))),
DEF("test:ck_store",
    S3(A("COMP"), A("test:ln_store"), A("test:ck_run"))),

"THE COMPOUND SCHEMES ARE READ ONCE PER STATE. read:state_mapinputs maps read:mi_row over every record, so whatever read:mi_row reaches runs once per record, and read:compounds there scanned the whole parse 1,256 times on a large app`s store. Lambda cannot count calls, but it can ask its own graph where a name is reached from, as test:unreach_ok does: this law holds when read:compounds and read:cmp_plans_c are reached from read:state_mapinputs, through read:cmp_ctx, and from nothing read:mi_row reaches. test:co_reach walks the definitions a name references, from the names given, to the fixed point. Before read:cmp_ctx, read:mi_row reached read:compounds through read:cmp_near, and this answered F.",
"test:co_refs: <<D, names>, name> to the names the definition of name references.",
DEF("test:co_refs",
