"THE VERDICT A COMPILE WRITES IS THE ONE THE WHOLE CHECK GIVES. A compile judged the store it wrote in a second process: build.js composed the serve module, booted it on the store and asked main:w2_clean, about 49 s on a large app. The compile judges it itself, over the closed store it already holds (compile:judge), before the store is put in place: the whole check when the store it replaces is not known clean or its constraints moved (compile:ip_known), and otherwise only the constraints the delta touched, by the argument of the write gate (Thm 1: a constraint that held of the store before and reads no population that moved holds of the store after). Over the clean fixture of test:w2_fx and the dirty one of test:w2_fx_bad, whose Function tagged b derives a W2Blocked its subset refuses: the judge told nothing answers what main:w2_clean answers of each; the judge told the clean store was known clean, given the delta to the dirty one (main:dl_moved, main:w2_types), answers what main:w2_clean answers of the dirty store, F, though the clean store met the subset; and over the clean store with nothing moved it answers T. compile:ip_known answers T only for a recorded alethic_clean T beside the stamp of the same constraints, and F for another stamp, for an F and for no record. compile:vschema, that stamp, is the same over the two fixtures, whose populations differ, and differs between test:w2_fx and the store it extends, whose constraints differ. Before compile:judge was defined it answered F, the subject being guarded for until then.",
"AND THE JUDGE IS THE SQL CHECKS, SO ITS FIXTURES ARE STORES A DATABASE HOLDS. compile:judge reads the database the compile wrote, so each fixture is written to one (test:cvd_db) as a compile writes its tables, and a store a compile writes is closed: test:w2_fx is the composed store with cells added by hand, not closed, and writing it took 18 s a time for its 78 tables, the derived rows of its stored derived fact types absent from them. The fixtures are now one readings file, as test:check_sql`s are: Crate is flagged, allowed, shipped and checked, Crate is blocked fully derived from Crate is flagged, a blocked Crate is allowed and a shipped Crate is checked. The clean store has c1 flagged and allowed and c2 shipped and checked; the dirty store has c1 flagged and not allowed, so it breaks the subset only through what is derived from it, which no table stores and the in-memory judge decides (check:res_sc); and a fourth store has c2 shipped and not checked, which the SQL checks decide: the judge told nothing answers of it what main:w2_clean answers, F. <store> to <the clean fixture, the dirty fixture, the store, the fixture the SQL checks find dirty>.",
