"A READ A SERVER ANSWERS FROM ITS DATABASE NEEDS NO POPULATION IN ITS CELLS. A server keeps each stored fact type as a cell of its rows, read of the tables the first time anything reaches into it, and every read verb read those cells. test:read_dry lays test:query_sql`s fixture out in law-read-dry.db as a compile lays it out, reads the store back as a server starts (test:qs_back), and takes from it every population: each cell named by a fact type the store declares -- but what was entered of a + fact type computed on read (test:rd_semi) -- FILE, and the rows of every descriptor of state:fts (test:rd_dry). The store in memory holds FILE as a server reading its tables on demand holds it (test:rd_filed), so the links of a GET are compared with what such a server answers. That store, served from the database and taken as a read takes it (store:for_read), answers every probe the store in memory answers, the same text: test:query_sql`s queries -- those the database answers and those lambda answers over populations it reads -- its gets, nav and orients, and ask of Member joins Club, actions of york, validate, the GET of Member joins Club whole and for bob, of the Function table whole and of the Function heads, with its links. Orient of heads answers its two Descriptions there, as in test:query_sql; test:orient_served holds the required leg. The database is made fresh before and cleared after. Run first, before a read read its populations in the database, it failed (unresolved atom store:for_read).",
